Committed text for the client that has the keyboard (Istesso's ADR 0007, "確定した文字", and
ADR 0020, route d): the compositor offers zwp_text_input_v3 to its clients, without an input
method, and the custom upstream event TextCommit (text=string) hands a piece of committed text to
the enabled text input of the client that has the keyboard (commit_string, then done). The text
inputs of a client are told they entered before its keyboard is. When the text input of the
window that got the keyboard has not enabled itself 0.3 s (and 1 s) later, that window is told
again that it has the keyboard (wl_keyboard leave and enter to that surface only; the seat's focus
does not change; once per surface, only while no key is down): the Text Editor (GTK 4.14), when it restores a page from its session, enables
its text input only after such a second focus (seen on the host). A client
without an enabled text input gets printable ASCII as key presses (US layout; on another layout
only letters, digits and the space; Caps Lock is taken into account); anything else is dropped. The text is never logged, only
how many characters went which way.

Composing text (Istesso's ADR 0007, "変換中の文字"): the upstream event TextPreedit (text,
cursor-begin, cursor-end as UTF-8 byte offsets) goes to the same text input as preedit_string and
done, so that the application draws it in its own font; empty text clears it. Never into a
password field (by its content type), whatever the device thought the field was. With the property
text-input-messages the element posts wayland.text-input (state: 0 no field, 1 a field, 2 a
password field by its content type; and the text cursor's rectangle in the output) whenever that
changes; no typed text is in it (ADR 0015, "文字の入力の欄").

A patch against gst-wayland-display (https://github.com/games-on-whales/gst-wayland-display,
MIT License), commit 1301f86, on top of gst-wayland-display-render-on-commit.patch,
gst-wayland-display-cursor-messages.patch, gst-wayland-display-windows.patch,
gst-wayland-display-dialogs.patch and gst-wayland-display-trial-windows.patch in this directory,
in that order (its name sorts after trial-windows on purpose). The lines of context are from that
project and from those patches.

FOR TRIALS AND EVERYDAY USE ONLY. It is not in the build the M1 gate and verdict measure, and it
does not go into main before a review (it touches the input path and input rights).

